# ══════════════════════ §23 · INSIDE THE ANALYZER ══════════════════════

# ── 1. Measured versus calculated (§23) ──────────────────────────────────────
q("A resident reviewing an abnormal complete blood count asks the laboratory which values on the panel the analyzer physically measures and which it derives arithmetically. The technologist explains that the instrument counts and sizes red cells by impedance as they cross a narrow aperture, and reads hemoglobin photometrically at 555 nm after lysis with sodium lauryl sulfate.\n\nWhich of the following reported values is directly measured rather than calculated?",
  ["A. Hematocrit, from the red cell count and the cell volume",
   "B. Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration, a hemoglobin density",
   "C. Mean corpuscular hemoglobin, from hemoglobin and cell count",
   "D. Mean corpuscular volume, taken from the impedance pulse height",
   "E. Red cell distribution width, from the spread of cell volumes"],
  3,
  "Three values on the panel are MEASURED: the red cell count and the MCV (both by impedance) and the hemoglobin (photometrically). Everything else is arithmetic:\n\n• Hct = (RBC × MCV) / 10\n• MCH = (Hgb / RBC) × 10\n• MCHC = (Hgb / Hct) × 100\n• RDW = standard deviation of volume / MCV\n\nThe impedance method is the Coulter principle: cells suspended in a conductive fluid are drawn through a narrow aperture, and each cell crossing changes the electrical impedance. EACH PULSE IS ONE CELL — that gives the count — and PULSE HEIGHT IS THAT CELL'S VOLUME, which gives the size.\n\nThis distinction is worth more than it first appears, because it explains nearly every artefact: CORRUPT ONE MEASURED VALUE AND EVERY NUMBER DERIVED FROM IT GOES WRONG AT THE SAME TIME. Cold agglutinins clump red cells, which lowers the count and raises the MCV, and the calculated hematocrit and MCHC both go wrong as a direct consequence. That is why an impossibly high MCHC is such a reliable alarm bell — it sits downstream of two other errors.\n\nThe other two methodologies on the panel: FLOW CYTOMETRY produces the white cell differential, separating cells by forward scatter (size) against side scatter (complexity and granularity); and the FLUORESCENT PLATELET CHANNEL stains platelet rough endoplasmic reticulum and mitochondria with oxazine, identifying platelets by what they contain rather than how big they are.",
  {"0": "The hematocrit is calculated as (RBC × MCV)/10. It replaced the spun hematocrit, which was a genuine physical measurement — the column of packed cells in a centrifuged tube — and that history is why many people still assume it is measured.",
   "2": "Mean corpuscular hemoglobin is calculated as (Hgb/RBC) × 10. It is an AMOUNT of hemoglobin per cell, which is the distinction from MCHC.",
   "1": "Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is calculated as (Hgb/Hct) × 100, so it is downstream of two other numbers. Being a concentration, it is bounded by physics — which is what makes it the best artefact detector on the panel.",
   "4": "Red cell distribution width is calculated as the standard deviation of the volume divided by the MCV. It is the width of the impedance curve expressed as a number, and it is what betrays two populations hiding under a normal average."},
  "The machine really only measures three things: how many red cells there are, how big they are, and how much hemoglobin is present. Every other number on the report is worked out from those three.",
  exim="fig_slide_coulter",
  excap="The Coulter principle: each cell crossing the aperture makes one pulse, and the height of that pulse is the cell's volume.")

# ── 2. Thalassemia versus iron deficiency at the CBC (§23) ───────────────────
q("A 24-year-old woman of Mediterranean ancestry is evaluated for a lifelong mild anemia found on routine testing. She has no fatigue, no heavy menstrual bleeding, and no gastrointestinal symptoms, and she has never taken iron. Her temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 72/min, and blood pressure is 114/70 mm Hg. Physical examination shows no pallor, no jaundice, and no splenomegaly.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.6 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 33% (N=36%–46%)\nErythrocyte count 6.1 million/mm3 (N=3.5–5.5 million/mm3)\nMean corpuscular volume 66 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 13.1% (N=11.4%–14.5%)\nSerum ferritin 96 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
  ["A. Anemia of chronic disease with an inflammatory block",
   "B. Beta-thalassemia trait",
   "C. Iron deficiency anemia from occult blood loss",
   "D. Lead poisoning with impaired heme synthesis",
   "E. Sideroblastic anemia from a marrow disorder"],
  1,
  "The discriminator is the ERYTHROCYTE COUNT, and it is the cleanest way to separate these two microcytic anemias at the complete blood count alone.\n\nBoth thalassemia and iron deficiency give a low hemoglobin with a low MCV. The difference is in how many cells the marrow makes. In THALASSEMIA the marrow makes MANY small cells — the algorithm notes explicitly that thalassemia may show an INCREASED red cell count, and hers is 6.1 million/mm3. In IRON DEFICIENCY the marrow makes FEW small cells, because it cannot build hemoglobin at all, so the count runs low.\n\nHer normal ferritin of 96 ng/mL confirms the direction, and her RDW of 13.1% fits too: thalassemia produces a uniformly small population, while iron deficiency typically widens the RDW as the population shifts.\n\nThe stake is not academic — THE TREATMENTS ARE OPPOSITE. Iron deficiency needs iron. A patient with thalassemia trait has normal or high iron stores, gains nothing from supplementation, and is put at risk of IRON OVERLOAD by it. Confirm with iron studies and HEMOGLOBIN ELECTROPHORESIS, because morphology alone cannot make this call.\n\nOne caution about the rule of three: hematocrit should be about 3 × hemoglobin, but that holds ONLY WHEN THE MCV IS NORMAL. Hers is 33% against a hemoglobin of 10.6 g/dL, which fits — but in a markedly microcytic patient a mismatch would point at cell size rather than at an error.",
  {"0": "Anemia of chronic disease is usually normocytic and becomes microcytic only later. It gives a LOW serum iron with a low iron-binding capacity and a normal or raised ferritin, and it requires an inflammatory illness, which she does not have.",
   "2": "Iron deficiency is the main alternative and the reason this question exists. It gives a LOW red cell count, a ferritin below 10 to 15 ng/mL and usually a raised RDW — the opposite of every number here.",
   "3": "Lead poisoning produces a microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling, and it belongs in the metal-toxicity branch of the microcytic column. There is no exposure history, no stippling, and it does not raise the red cell count.",
   "4": "Sideroblastic anemia is bracketed in the microcytic column and listed in the normocytic one, since it often starts normocytic. It comes with a HIGH ferritin and ringed sideroblasts on marrow iron staining, and it does not produce erythrocytosis."},
  "Two conditions make small red cells. In one the body cannot get the ingredients, so it makes few small cells. In hers the factory works fine but the recipe is wrong, so it makes lots of small cells. The cell count tells them apart.",
  exim="fig_slide_anemia_algorithm",
  excap="The anemia algorithm as the analyzer hands it to you — split on MCV first, then on the reticulocyte count in the normocytic column.")

# ── 3. The reticulocyte branch point (§23) ──────────────────────────────────
q("A 58-year-old man is evaluated for anemia found during a preoperative assessment. He has no bleeding, no weight loss, and no known chronic illness. His temperature is 36.9°C (98.4°F), pulse is 80/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows mild conjunctival pallor and no organomegaly.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.4 g/dL (N=13.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 28% (N=37%–49%)\nMean corpuscular volume 89 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 13.8% (N=11.4%–14.5%)\nLeukocyte count 6,800/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 232,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\n\nWhich of the following tests will most efficiently divide the differential diagnosis?",
  ["A. Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy",
   "B. Hemoglobin electrophoresis with quantitation",
   "C. Reticulocyte count",
   "D. Serum erythropoietin concentration",
   "E. Serum ferritin and iron binding capacity"],
  2,
  "In the NORMOCYTIC column the reticulocyte count is the branch point, and it answers the only question that matters at this stage: IS THIS LOW PRODUCTION OR INCREASED DESTRUCTION? Normal range roughly 0.8% to 4%.\n\n• HIGH reticulocytes — the marrow is working, so cells are being lost: hemolysis, recovery from an insult, hemoglobinopathies, enzymopathies, membranopathies.\n• LOW reticulocytes — the marrow itself is the problem: renal failure (no erythropoietin), chronic disease, sideroblastic anemia, hypoendocrine states, aplastic anemia, or a mixed deficiency.\n\nThe algorithm is built in that order for a reason: start with a low hemoglobin, split on the MCV, and split again on the reticulocyte count in the normocytic column only. His MCV of 89 µm3 puts him squarely in that column, and the reticulocyte count is inexpensive, fast, and divides the list in half.\n\nWhy a reticulocyte count works at all is worth understanding rather than memorizing. A red cell is born with a nucleus because it needs one to manufacture hemoglobin; once loaded, the nucleus is ejected. A reticulocyte is the stage just after that — no nucleus, but still carrying leftover RNA, which is exactly what the reticulocyte stain detects. A mature red cell has neither.\n\nThe same logic has a platelet version: the IMMATURE PLATELET FRACTION is the reticulocyte count for platelets, and both answer 'is the marrow producing?' before the mature count moves.",
  {"0": "A marrow examination answers the question definitively and would be the eventual step for an unexplained hypoproliferative anemia. It is invasive and expensive as a FIRST move, and in many patients the reticulocyte count and iron studies make it unnecessary.",
   "1": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis is the test for the hemoglobinopathies, and the single most actionable next step when target cells appear on a smear. His cells are normocytic and no abnormal morphology is described.",
   "3": "A serum erythropoietin level is useful in specific situations, such as separating a secondary erythrocytosis from polycythemia vera, or judging whether an anemic patient with renal disease is under-producing. It does not divide a normocytic differential the way a reticulocyte count does.",
   "4": "Iron studies are the right test in the MICROCYTIC column and remain worth sending here, since iron deficiency can be normocytic early. They address one branch of the differential rather than splitting the whole list into production versus destruction."},
  "His red cells are a normal size, which leaves two broad possibilities: he is not making enough, or he is destroying them too fast. One cheap test — counting the brand-new cells — tells you which.",
  exim="fig_slide_retic_stain",
  excap="Reticulocyte staining: the RNA left behind after the nucleus is ejected is what the stain detects.")

# ── 4. Accuracy versus precision (§23) ──────────────────────────────────────
q("A hematology laboratory performs daily quality control. Over 3 weeks, the automated platelet count on every quality control material reads approximately 15% LOWER than the assigned reference value, with very little scatter between runs. Red cell and white cell parameters are within limits. The instrument has not been recalibrated since a recent service visit.\n\nWhich of the following best characterizes this problem?",
  ["A. Decreased accuracy from random bias, which cannot be corrected",
   "B. Decreased accuracy from systematic bias, correctable by calibration",
   "C. Decreased precision from random bias, intrinsic to the assay",
   "D. Decreased precision from systematic bias, correctable by calibration",
   "E. Expected analytic variation, since platelets vary between runs"],
  1,
  "Results that are off by a CONSISTENT AMOUNT IN A CONSISTENT DIRECTION are the definition of SYSTEMATIC BIAS, and systematic bias reduces ACCURACY. It often represents an instrument problem and is fixable by CALIBRATION — which is exactly what the service visit and the missing recalibration point to.\n\nThe two rules of thumb are worth carrying verbatim:\n• CONSISTENTLY WRONG → suspect the machine. Systematic bias, decreased accuracy, fix by calibration.\n• INCONSISTENTLY WRONG → suspect the method. Random bias, decreased precision, and it may be an INTRINSIC FEATURE OF THE ASSAY that cannot be engineered away.\n\nThe detail that makes this practical: PRECISION IS PARTLY A PROPERTY OF THE ANALYTE, NOT JUST THE MACHINE. Platelets fluctuate by roughly 15% to 20% between repeat runs intrinsically, because they activate — so a repeat count differing by 15% is not an instrument fault. The MCV, by contrast, is very tightly precise, so a genuinely shifting MCV means something real.\n\nThe trap in this vignette is that 15% is exactly the magnitude of normal platelet imprecision. What rules that out is the pattern: the deviation is in ONE DIRECTION EVERY TIME, with very little scatter. Random biological variation scatters around the truth; this sits off to one side of it.\n\nOne more definition that belongs here: turnaround time is measured across the WHOLE WORKFLOW — sample arrival to result reaching the clinician — not just the instrument's run time.",
  {"0": "Random bias reduces precision, not accuracy, and the two terms are not interchangeable. Random error scatters results around the true value rather than displacing them consistently to one side.",
   "2": "This is what would be seen if the counts varied widely in both directions around the reference value. It is also the honest description of ordinary platelet imprecision — but that is scatter, and this is displacement.",
   "3": "The pairing is wrong in both halves: systematic bias affects accuracy rather than precision. This option exists to test whether the two axes are being read independently.",
   "4": "Platelets genuinely do vary 15% to 20% between runs, which makes this the strongest distractor. Biological variation is random and scatters in both directions; a 15% shortfall on EVERY control, with little scatter, is a calibration problem."},
  "Every reading is wrong by the same amount in the same direction. That is the machine being off, not the test being noisy — and a machine that is consistently off can be recalibrated.",
  exim="fig_slide_accuracy_precision",
  excap="Accuracy versus precision: systematic bias moves every shot off-centre; random bias scatters them.")

# ── 5. Reading the scattergram (§23) ─────────────────────────────────────────
q("A laboratory technologist is explaining the automated differential to a student. The analyzer separates leukocytes by FORWARD scatter, which reflects cell size, plotted against SIDE scatter, which reflects internal complexity and granularity. The student is asked to predict where each normal leukocyte population will fall on the plot.\n\nWhich of the following cells will show the greatest SIDE scatter?",
  ["A. Basophil",
   "B. Eosinophil",
   "C. Lymphocyte",
   "D. Monocyte",
   "E. Neutrophil"],
  1,
  "Side scatter reports INTERNAL COMPLEXITY AND GRANULARITY, and the eosinophil — with its very bright granules and a lobed nucleus — is pushed FAR RIGHT on that axis. Nothing else in the normal differential is as granular.\n\nThe rest of the map:\n• LYMPHOCYTE — a simple cell: spherical nucleus, thin rim of cytoplasm. Low side scatter, low fluorescence, so it sits BOTTOM-LEFT.\n• MONOCYTE — the largest of the normal white cells, so high forward scatter puts it HIGH UP.\n• GHOST CELLS — cells that lysed during collection or processing. Having lost their contents and surface markers, they show low forward and low side scatter and are gated out as debris. A lot of them means a degraded sample.\n\nThose two axes are what let the machine tell a lymphocyte from a neutrophil at all — the differential comes from FLOW CYTOMETRY, not from impedance, which is a distinction worth keeping straight.\n\nThis is also how the 'abnormal scattergram' and 'blasts?' flags are produced: blasts and immature cells are larger and cluster somewhere the normal populations do not, and a dense cloud in unexpected territory pulls the slide out of AUTOVERIFICATION and onto a microscope.\n\nThe precision argument for all of this: the analyzer counts about 36,000 CELLS PER SAMPLE, while a technologist performing a manual differential counts 100 TO 200. Automation is not about speed for its own sake — it frees technologists to spend their time on smear review for atypical cells such as leukemic blasts.",
  {"0": "Basophils are granular too, and their granules take up basic dye and stain dark blue-purple. They are scarce in normal blood and are not the population that defines the high side-scatter corner of the plot.",
   "2": "The lymphocyte is the simplest normal leukocyte — a spherical nucleus with a thin rim of cytoplasm — so it has LOW side scatter and low forward scatter, and sits in the bottom-left corner.",
   "3": "The monocyte is the LARGEST normal white cell, so it sits high on the forward-scatter axis. Size and granularity are different axes, which is the point of the two-dimensional plot.",
   "4": "Neutrophils are granulated and do sit to the right of lymphocytes, but their granules are the ones indifferent to both dyes, staining a neutral pale lilac. The eosinophil's granules scatter light far more strongly."},
  "The machine shines a light at each cell. Size bends the light one way; internal graininess scatters it another. The cell stuffed with the brightest granules scatters the most.",
  exim="fig_slide_scattergram",
  excap="Scattergram displays from an automated counter. A dense cloud where no normal population belongs is what generates a 'blasts?' flag.")

# ── 6. The immature platelet fraction (§23) ──────────────────────────────────
q("A 46-year-old woman is on day 16 after an allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplant for acute myeloid leukemia. She has required platelet transfusions twice weekly. Her temperature is 36.9°C (98.4°F), pulse is 84/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. She has no bleeding and no petechiae. Her last platelet transfusion was 4 days ago.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8.6 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 26% (N=36%–46%)\nLeukocyte count 1,400/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 14,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nImmature platelet fraction 9.8% (N=1.0%–5.0%)\n\nWhich of the following does the immature platelet fraction indicate in this patient?",
  ["A. Consumption of platelets by an immune mechanism",
   "B. Graft failure requiring a second transplant",
   "C. Platelet clumping producing a falsely low count",
   "D. Recovering marrow production, with a rise expected shortly",
   "E. Relapse of acute myeloid leukemia in the marrow"],
  3,
  "The immature platelet fraction is the RETICULOCYTE COUNT FOR PLATELETS — same logic, different lineage. A reticulocyte is a red cell with residual RNA; an immature platelet is a newly released platelet still carrying rough endoplasmic reticulum and mitochondria, which is what the fluorescent stain detects.\n\nIts named clinical use is exactly this situation: MONITORING ENGRAFTMENT AFTER A STEM CELL OR MARROW TRANSPLANT. The platelet count is still low, but if IMMATURE PLATELETS START APPEARING, the new marrow has begun producing — and the actual count will start to rise in TWO OR THREE DAYS. That is days of warning you would not otherwise have, in a setting where the alternative is watching a flat count and wondering.\n\nBoth markers answer the same question before the mature count moves: IS THE MARROW PRODUCING? A high fraction means new cells are being released; a low one means the production problem has not resolved.\n\nThe same fluorescent platelet channel has a second job worth knowing. Because it identifies platelets by WHAT THEY CONTAIN rather than how big they are, it rescues counts that impedance sizing gets wrong — giant platelets missed because they are too large, and microcytes or schistocytes counted as platelets because they are small enough to fall into the window. It correlates with CD41/CD61 flow cytometry and is especially useful below 50,000/mm3.",
  {"0": "Immune destruction consumes platelets in the periphery, and the marrow responds by releasing young platelets — so the fraction can be high there too. It does not fit a day-16 transplant patient with pancytopenia and no bleeding, where the marrow has been empty by design.",
   "1": "Graft failure is the feared complication at this point, and it is what a persistently absent immature platelet fraction would suggest. A rising fraction is the opposite finding — evidence that donor marrow has started to work.",
   "2": "Clumping produces a falsely low count and is confirmed by looking at the smear, then resolved by redrawing in citrate. It would not raise the immature platelet fraction, and her count is genuinely low after a transplant.",
   "4": "Relapse would be signalled by blasts in the blood or marrow and by failure of counts to recover. A marker of new platelet production is not a relapse marker."},
  "The count is still low, but the machine can see that brand-new platelets are appearing. New platelets mean the transplanted marrow has switched on, and the number should start climbing in a couple of days.")

# ── 7. The averaging trap (§23) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
q("A 62-year-old man with chronic gastrointestinal blood loss from angiodysplasia was transfused 3 units of packed red blood cells 5 days ago. Before transfusion his hemoglobin was 6.8 g/dL with a mean corpuscular volume of 68 µm3. He feels better and has had no further bleeding. His temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 82/min, and blood pressure is 126/74 mm Hg.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (N=13.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 29% (N=37%–49%)\nMean corpuscular volume 86 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 21.4% (N=11.4%–14.5%)\nSerum ferritin 7 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\n\nWhich of the following best explains the mean corpuscular volume?",
  ["A. A single population of cells with increased hemoglobin content",
   "B. A single uniform population of normal-sized erythrocytes",
   "C. Cold agglutinins clumping cells and inflating measured volume",
   "D. Two erythrocyte populations of different sizes, averaged together",
   "E. Under-filling of the collection tube, diluting the specimen"],
  3,
  "The MCV is an AVERAGE, and averages hide bimodal data. Two scenarios are named for producing a normal MCV over two abnormal populations:\n\n• A microcytic anemia with a BRISK RETICULOCYTOSIS — small cells averaged with large young ones.\n• AFTER TRANSFUSING NORMAL BLOOD INTO A MICROCYTIC PATIENT — the patient's small cells averaged with the donor's normal ones.\n\nThis man is the second scenario exactly. His own cells are still at 68 µm3, the donor's are normal, and the reported 86 µm3 describes no cell actually present in his circulation.\n\nThe tell is a RAISED RDW WITH A NORMAL MCV. Red cell distribution width is the standard deviation of volume divided by the MCV — literally the width of the impedance curve — so it rises when two populations sit side by side. His is 21.4%, far above the 11.4% to 14.5% range, and his ferritin of 7 ng/mL confirms that the underlying iron deficiency is untreated.\n\nThe confirmation is LOOKING AT THE SMEAR, where two populations are directly visible. This is the strongest single argument for why the smear still exists in an age of automation — the analyzer reports a number that is arithmetically correct and clinically misleading, and only the slide shows why.\n\nManagement follows: his iron deficiency still needs treating, and transfusion has corrected the symptom without touching the cause.",
  {"0": "Increased hemoglobin content per cell is a raised MCH, which is a different index and would not move the MCV on its own. Nothing here suggests a hyperdense population.",
   "1": "This is what a normal MCV would ordinarily mean, and it is why the number is misleading. A genuinely uniform population would have a NORMAL RDW; his is 21.4%.",
   "2": "Cold agglutinins do inflate the MCV, because clumped cells cross the aperture together and are counted as one large cell. That artefact comes with a falsely low red cell count and an impossibly high MCHC, neither of which is present.",
   "4": "An under-filled tube gives excess EDTA relative to blood, which shrinks red cells and LOWERS the MCV and hematocrit. It is a real preanalytic error, but it does not create a wide RDW in a recently transfused patient."},
  "He has two sets of red cells in him — his own small ones and the normal-sized donated ones. The machine reports the average, which lands in the normal range even though not one of his cells is that size.",
  exim="fig_slide_anemia_algorithm",
  excap="The algorithm starts by splitting on MCV — which is why an averaged MCV can send the whole workup down the wrong column.")

# ══════════════════════ §24 · THE PERIPHERAL SMEAR ══════════════════════

# ── 8. The feather edge artefact (§24) ───────────────────────────────────────
q("A first-year technologist reviews a peripheral smear on a 40-year-old woman with a mild normocytic anemia and reports numerous spherocytes. The supervising technologist observes that the assessment was made at the thin trailing end of the slide, where the cells are widely separated and the larger cells have collected. The automated indices show a mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration of 33.4 g/dL (N=31–37 g/dL), and the reticulocyte count is 1.4% (N=0.8%–4.0%). The patient has no jaundice and no splenomegaly.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
  ["A. Order a direct antiglobulin test and osmotic fragility testing",
   "B. Reassess the morphology in the monolayer of the smear",
   "C. Repeat the complete blood count on a citrated specimen",
   "D. Report hereditary spherocytosis and refer to hematology",
   "E. Warm the specimen to 37°C and repeat the analysis"],
  1,
  "Morphology must be read in the MONOLAYER — the region where cells are evenly distributed WITHOUT TOUCHING OR OVERLAPPING. There are three regions on a smear and only one of them is interpretable:\n\n• THE FEATHER EDGE — the thin trailing end. Cells are spread too far apart and DISTORTED, and LARGE CELLS PREFERENTIALLY COLLECT HERE. Red cells lose their central pallor as an ARTEFACT of being flattened and over-spread — which is precisely the feature that defines a spherocyte. Reading here MANUFACTURES SPHEROCYTES THAT ARE NOT PRESENT.\n• THE THICKER EDGE — cells overlap and pile up, uninterpretable for the opposite reason.\n• THE MONOLAYER — between the two, and the only place morphology should be assessed.\n\nHer numbers support the correction rather than the original report. Real spherocytosis is a hemolytic state: it should raise the reticulocyte count, and in hereditary spherocytosis the cells are genuinely hyperdense, so the MCHC is typically HIGH — that is the one setting in which a high MCHC is a diagnostic clue rather than an error. Hers is 33.4 g/dL with a reticulocyte count of 1.4%, and she has no jaundice or splenomegaly.\n\nA related practical point: a consistently made smear matters because automated image-capture systems CANNOT COMPENSATE FOR A BADLY MADE SLIDE, and neither can a human. That is the argument for the automated spreader.\n\nWhat a normal red cell looks like, for reference: a donut whose hole is filled in — the centre is THINNER, not absent, and that dip reads as the central pallor, normally about ONE THIRD of the cell. Notably wider than that is hypochromia.",
  {"0": "These are the confirmatory tests for spherocytosis — a direct antiglobulin test to separate immune from hereditary causes, and osmotic fragility for the hereditary form. Ordering them commits to a finding that has not been established on a valid part of the slide.",
   "2": "A citrated redraw is the fix for EDTA-dependent platelet clumping, where the responsible antibody requires EDTA. It has no bearing on red cell morphology or on where the slide was read.",
   "3": "Reporting the diagnosis skips both the correction and the confirmation. Her reticulocyte count and MCHC do not support a hemolytic state, and no family history or clinical findings are described.",
   "4": "Warming to 37°C disperses cold agglutinins and is the fix for red cell agglutination. Agglutination would show a falsely low red cell count with an impossibly high MCHC, which is not this panel."},
  "The very edge of a blood film squashes red cells flat and rubs out their pale centres, which makes normal cells look like a specific abnormal cell. You have to look at the part of the slide where the cells sit in a single even layer.",
  exim="fig_slide_smear_regions",
  excap="Smear preparation and the regions of a film. Morphology is read in the monolayer — never at the feather edge.")

# ── 9. Burr cells versus spur cells (§24) ────────────────────────────────────
q("A 54-year-old man is evaluated for fatigue. His serum creatinine is 6.8 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL) and his blood urea nitrogen is 82 mg/dL (N=7–20 mg/dL). His liver enzymes and bilirubin are normal, and there is no history of alcohol use. A peripheral smear reviewed in the monolayer shows numerous red cells bearing MANY SHORT, REGULAR, EVENLY SPACED projections around the entire circumference. The requesting clinician telephones to say the finding confirms occult liver disease.\n\nWhich of the following is the most accurate response?",
  ["A. The cells are acanthocytes, which indicate severe liver disease",
   "B. The cells are echinocytes, seen in uremia or as an artefact",
   "C. The cells are schistocytes, which indicate a microangiopathy",
   "D. The cells are spherocytes, which indicate immune hemolysis",
   "E. The cells are target cells, which indicate a hemoglobinopathy"],
  1,
  "MANY, REGULAR, EVENLY SPACED spicules describe an ECHINOCYTE — the burr cell. Echinocytes point toward UREMIA AND RENAL FAILURE, which this man has with a creatinine of 6.8 mg/dL, or toward a PREPARATION ARTEFACT from slow drying, prolonged storage before smearing, or a stain pH problem.\n\nThe liver disease cell is the ACANTHOCYTE — the spur cell — which has FEW, IRREGULAR, UNEVENLY SPACED blunt projections, and is seen in severe liver disease, abetalipoproteinemia, and after splenectomy.\n\nThe shortest version of the rule: BURR CELLS POINT TO KIDNEY, SPUR CELLS POINT TO LIVER. And the spelling hook that makes it stick: ACanthocyte has Asymmetric spicules — few and irregular, so liver. EChinocyte has Even ones — many and regular, so kidney, or an artefact.\n\nThat last possibility is the practical reason to tell them apart: ONE IS A FINDING, THE OTHER IS OFTEN JUST A BADLY HANDLED SLIDE. So the response to this clinician has two halves — the cells point at the kidney, not the liver, and it is worth checking when and how the slide was made. If the sample sat before smearing, request a fresh specimen.\n\nHis normal liver enzymes and bilirubin, with a markedly raised creatinine and urea, make the renal reading the one the whole panel supports.",
  {"0": "The acanthocyte is exactly the cell the clinician has in mind, and it would support their conclusion — but it has FEW, IRREGULAR, UNEVENLY SPACED projections, not many even ones. His liver enzymes and bilirubin are also normal.",
   "2": "Schistocytes are FRAGMENTS with two pointed ends and no central pallor, and they signal microangiopathy — thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic uremic syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, malignant hypertension, mechanical valves. Fragments and spiculated intact cells are different objects.",
   "3": "Spherocytes are small, round and densely stained with NO central pallor and no projections at all. They occur in hereditary spherocytosis and autoimmune hemolysis, where splenic macrophages remove membrane.",
   "4": "Target cells have a bullseye — a central dot of hemoglobin within the pallor — and reflect excess membrane relative to contents. They are a feature of thalassemia, hemoglobin C, liver disease and the post-splenectomy state."},
  "Two kinds of spiky red cell look similar. The one with many even spikes goes with kidney failure — or with a badly prepared slide. The one with a few irregular spikes goes with liver disease. This patient's kidneys are failing.",
  exim="fig_slide_morph_panel",
  excap="Target cells, hemoglobin C crystals, acanthocytes and echinocytes side by side — the spiky pair is the one worth separating on sight.")

# ── 10. Target cells and the next test (§24) ─────────────────────────────────
q("A 21-year-old man is evaluated for a mild anemia present since childhood. He has no symptoms and takes no medications. He drinks no alcohol, and he has not had a splenectomy. His temperature is 36.7°C (98.1°F), pulse is 68/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Physical examination shows no jaundice, no hepatomegaly, and no splenomegaly. A peripheral smear is shown.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 11.2 g/dL (N=13.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 34% (N=37%–49%)\nMean corpuscular volume 71 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum ferritin 142 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\nTransferrin saturation 34% (N=20%–50%)\nAspartate aminotransferase 22 U/L (N=10–40 U/L)\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next test?",
  ["A. Bone marrow aspiration with iron staining",
   "B. Direct antiglobulin test on a fresh specimen",
   "C. Hemoglobin electrophoresis",
   "D. Osmotic fragility testing of washed red cells",
   "E. Repeat iron studies after 3 months of oral iron"],
  2,
  "Target cells with a microcytosis and NORMAL iron studies means a hemoglobin disorder, and the single most actionable next step is HEMOGLOBIN ELECTROPHORESIS.\n\nAsked directly whether thalassemia can be identified from the smear, the answer was a clear NO — YOU CANNOT DIFFERENTIATE THESE CONDITIONS FROM MORPHOLOGY ALONE. Target cells are a feature of hemoglobin disorders generally, thalassemias and hemoglobinopathies alike, and COMBINATIONS EXIST: hemoglobin S with beta thalassemia, hemoglobin S with hemoglobin C. Only electrophoresis separates them.\n\nThe mechanism behind the shape is worth knowing because it explains the whole differential. A target cell has TOO MUCH MEMBRANE FOR THE HEMOGLOBIN INSIDE, so the excess surface folds into a central bump. That happens either because the CONTENTS ARE REDUCED — thalassemia, iron deficiency — or because the MEMBRANE IS INCREASED — liver disease, where lipid loading expands it, and the post-splenectomy state, where the spleen is no longer trimming cells. Same final shape, opposite causes.\n\nThis vignette closes off the membrane-side causes deliberately: his liver enzymes are normal, he drinks no alcohol, and he has a spleen. And it closes off the commonest contents-side cause too, since his ferritin is 142 ng/mL with a transferrin saturation of 34%.\n\nWhat is left is a lifelong microcytosis with normal iron — which is thalassemia trait or a structural variant, and electrophoresis is the test that names it.",
  {"0": "Marrow examination with iron staining is how ringed sideroblasts are found, and it would be reasonable for an unexplained microcytosis after simpler tests. It is invasive, and a blood test answers the question here.",
   "1": "A direct antiglobulin test detects antibody or complement on red cells and is the test for immune hemolysis. He has no evidence of hemolysis, and target cells are not an immune finding.",
   "3": "Osmotic fragility testing is for hereditary spherocytosis, where cells lyse in hypotonic saline more readily than normal. Target cells have the opposite problem — an excess of membrane — and are osmotically resistant.",
   "4": "A therapeutic trial of iron is the right move in a microcytic patient with LOW iron stores. His ferritin and transferrin saturation are normal, so iron will do nothing except risk overload, which is the specific hazard of treating thalassemia trait as iron deficiency."},
  "His red cells look like little bullseyes and are small, but his iron is completely normal — so the problem is the hemoglobin itself. A smear cannot say which hemoglobin disorder it is; one test can.",
  image="fig_target_cells",
  imcap="Peripheral blood smear, monolayer.",
  exim="fig_slide_morph_panel",
  excap="The morphology panel: target cells result from excess membrane relative to hemoglobin content.")

# ── 11. Schistocytes and the coagulation screen (§24) ────────────────────────
q("A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of bruising, confusion, and fever for 2 days. She takes no medications and is not pregnant. Her temperature is 38.6°C (101.5°F), pulse is 108/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. She is disoriented to place and time. Physical examination shows scattered petechiae and no focal neurologic deficit. A peripheral smear is shown.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8.2 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 25% (N=36%–46%)\nPlatelet count 18,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nReticulocyte count 7.2% (N=0.8%–4.0%)\nLactate dehydrogenase 980 U/L (N=100–220 U/L)\nHaptoglobin <10 mg/dL (N=30–200 mg/dL)\nSerum creatinine 1.1 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL)\nProthrombin time 12 seconds (N=11–15 seconds)\nActivated partial thromboplastin time 29 seconds (N=25–35 seconds)\nFibrinogen 340 mg/dL (N=200–400 mg/dL)\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
  ["A. Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
   "B. Hemolytic uremic syndrome",
   "C. Immune thrombocytopenia",
   "D. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura",
   "E. Vitamin B12 deficiency with pseudothrombotic microangiopathy"],
  3,
  "Schistocytes narrow the field but do not close it — THE COAGULATION PANEL DOES.\n\nSchistocytes appear in EVERY entry on the microangiopathy list: thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic uremic syndrome, atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, malignant hypertension, mechanical valves, march hemoglobinuria, and obstetric complications. The smear cannot make the distinction. The discriminator is the clotting screen:\n\n• TTP, HUS, aHUS — PT and aPTT NORMAL, fibrinogen NORMAL. The clotting factors are not being consumed; the problem is platelets and endothelium.\n• DIC — PT and aPTT BOTH PROLONGED, fibrinogen LOW OR FALLING, D-dimer markedly raised.\n\nHer PT is 12 seconds, aPTT 29 seconds, fibrinogen 340 mg/dL — all normal, which removes disseminated intravascular coagulation.\n\nWithin the thrombotic microangiopathies, the FEVER and the ACUTE MENTAL STATUS CHANGE with a NORMAL CREATININE point to thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura rather than hemolytic uremic syndrome, where renal failure dominates. Mechanistically this is ADAMTS13 failure, leaving ultra-large von Willebrand multimers to snag platelets across the microvasculature.\n\nThe rest of her panel is the hemolysis set moving as a unit: a reticulocyte count of 7.2%, an LDH of 980 U/L, and an unmeasurable haptoglobin. This is destruction, not underproduction — and the fragments on the slide say it is mechanical.",
  {"0": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation is the single most important alternative, and it produces identical fragments on the smear. It consumes clotting factors, so the PT and aPTT prolong and the fibrinogen falls, with a markedly raised D-dimer. All three are normal here.",
   "1": "Hemolytic uremic syndrome shares the normal coagulation screen and is the closest relative of the right answer. It is dominated by ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY, usually after a diarrheal illness in a child; her creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL and renal function is intact.",
   "2": "Immune thrombocytopenia causes isolated thrombocytopenia with bruising and petechiae, and would fit the platelet count alone. It does not cause hemolysis, fragments, fever or confusion, and her hemoglobin and hemolysis markers are clearly abnormal.",
   "4": "Severe vitamin B12 deficiency can mimic a microangiopathy, with a high LDH, a low haptoglobin and even fragments, which is why it is worth knowing. It produces a MACROCYTIC anemia with a LOW reticulocyte count, whereas hers is 7.2%, and the picture develops over months rather than 2 days."},
  "Her red cells are being shredded as they squeeze past clots in tiny vessels, and the clots are using up her platelets. The clotting tests are normal, which rules out the condition where the whole clotting system is being consumed.",
  image="fig_schistocytes",
  imcap="Peripheral blood smear at high power.")

# ── 12. Species identification in malaria (§24) ──────────────────────────────
q("A 5-year-old boy who returned from Afghanistan 6 weeks ago is brought to the emergency department because of recurring fevers. His mother reports numerous mosquito bites during the trip. His temperature is 39.1°C (102.4°F), pulse is 132/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a palpable spleen tip.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.7 g/dL (N=11.5–15.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 32% (N=35%–45%)\nMean corpuscular volume 70 µm3 (N=77–95 µm3)\nPlatelet count 47,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\n\nA Giemsa-stained smear shows intraerythrocytic parasites with a parasite load of 1.8%; mature schizonts are seen. A rapid antigen test shows a band for the Plasmodium vivax, P. malariae, and P. ovale common antigen, and the P. falciparum band is negative. Blood-stage therapy is begun. Additional therapy directed at the liver is required for which of the following reasons?",
  ["A. Dormant hepatic hypnozoites can cause later relapse",
   "B. Gametocytes persist in the circulation after treatment",
   "C. Hepatic schizonts are resistant to blood-stage drugs",
   "D. Parasites sequester in the spleen and reseed the blood",
   "E. Reinfection is likely from untreated household contacts"],
  0,
  "Plasmodium vivax and P. ovale form DORMANT LIVER HYPNOZOITES, so treatment must additionally target RELAPSE, not just the blood stage. Clearing the parasites visible on the smear leaves a reservoir in the liver that can reactivate weeks to months later.\n\nEverything in the vignette identifies the species. The rapid antigen test shows the vivax/malariae/ovale common antigen band with the FALCIPARUM BAND NEGATIVE — species identification changes management, which is why the test is read as bands rather than as positive or negative. The morphology fits: MATURE SCHIZONTS, the parasite progressively taking over the cell and consuming its hemoglobin. And P. vivax is milder and more common in Afghanistan.\n\nThe parasite load is reported AS A PERCENTAGE, and that is not optional: it quantifies severity, drives treatment intensity, and is followed to judge response. His is 1.8%. P. FALCIPARUM can present ABOVE 10%, with severe cases running 15% to 20%, because it infects RED CELLS OF ALL AGES, whereas P. vivax prefers young cells, which caps its parasite load. Falciparum is also the one that causes cerebral malaria and death.\n\nThe pattern that should trigger a malaria smear in the first place: FEVER + TRAVEL + THROMBOCYTOPENIA. Thrombocytopenia is present in the large majority of cases and is among the most consistent laboratory clues — his platelet count of 47,000/mm3 is the number that stands out on this panel.",
  {"1": "Gametocytes are the stage a feeding mosquito takes up, and clearing them matters for interrupting TRANSMISSION rather than for protecting this patient. Relapse in vivax comes from the liver, not from circulating gametocytes.",
   "2": "Hepatic schizonts are the actively dividing liver stage that precedes the blood stage in every species, including falciparum, and they are not what makes vivax relapse. The dormant form is the issue, and it is specific to vivax and ovale.",
   "3": "Splenic sequestration is not the mechanism of malarial relapse; his palpable spleen reflects the work it is doing clearing parasitized cells. Falciparum does sequester, but in the deep microvasculature rather than as a reservoir for later relapse.",
   "4": "Reinfection requires another infected mosquito bite, and he is no longer in an endemic area. Relapse from a dormant liver stage occurs with no new exposure at all, which is exactly what makes it a treatment problem."},
  "The medicine kills the parasites in his blood, but this species also leaves sleeping copies hiding in the liver. If those are not treated too, they wake up weeks later and the illness comes back.",
  exim="fig_pvivax_smear",
  excap="Lecture slide: the case's parasite load and rapid antigen result, with the P. vivax life-cycle forms.")

# ── 13. The toddler, the smear, and the milk (§24) ───────────────────────────
q("An 18-month-old boy is brought to the office because of fatigue and pallor. His mother reports that he drinks about 40 ounces of whole cow's milk daily and eats little solid food. He was born at term. His temperature is 36.9°C (98.4°F), pulse is 128/min, and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. Physical examination shows marked pallor and a soft systolic murmur. A peripheral smear is shown.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 3.6 g/dL (N=10.5–13.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 11% (N=33%–39%)\nMean corpuscular volume 58 µm3 (N=70–86 µm3)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 421 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nSerum ferritin 1 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\n\nHemoglobin electrophoresis is normal. In addition to transfusion and oral iron, his mother is advised to restrict cow's milk. Which of the following best explains that advice?",
  ["A. Milk calcium is deposited in the marrow, impairing erythropoiesis",
   "B. Milk fat causes lipemia that interferes with hemoglobin measurement",
   "C. Milk is low in iron, impairs its absorption, and can cause blood loss",
   "D. Milk protein triggers an autoimmune hemolytic anemia in toddlers",
   "E. Undiagnosed lactose intolerance causes diarrhea and iron loss"],
  2,
  "Excessive cow's milk in a toddler causes iron deficiency THREE SEPARATE WAYS: it is LOW IN IRON; its CALCIUM AND CASEIN INHIBIT ABSORPTION of what iron there is; and it can cause OCCULT GASTROINTESTINAL BLOOD LOSS. Giving iron without addressing the milk treats the number rather than the cause.\n\nThe smear is the classic one for this case — microcytic, hypochromic cells with WIDE CENTRAL PALLOR. Normal central pallor occupies about ONE THIRD of the cell; notably wider than that is hypochromia, which is the concrete threshold behind the vague phrase 'faint staining.'\n\nHis iron studies are the matched set: a total iron-binding capacity of 421 µg/dL (high — more transferrin hunting for iron) with a ferritin of 1 ng/mL (stores essentially absent).\n\nTwo numbers worth carrying from this case. Roughly 10 TO 15 cc/kg OF RED CELLS RAISES HEMOGLOBIN BY ABOUT 2 TO 3 g/dL. And the transfusion target is CLOSER TO 7, NOT NORMAL — in a chronic, slowly developing anemia the child has physiologically compensated, so transfusion is deliberately partial and cautious, and the marrow does the rest once given iron.\n\nHis hematocrit of 11% against a hemoglobin of 3.6 g/dL is worth noticing too: it fits the rule of three, which holds when you remember the qualifier — the relationship depends on cell size, and in profound microcytosis the numbers still track because both are driven by the same deficit.",
  {"0": "Calcium in milk does interfere with iron, which makes this half-right and therefore tempting — but it acts in the GUT LUMEN, competing at absorption, not by depositing in marrow. There is no marrow deposition mechanism here.",
   "1": "Lipemia is a genuine laboratory interference that falsely raises the measured hemoglobin and therefore the MCH and MCHC. Dietary milk does not produce lipemic plasma, and his low hemoglobin is real rather than artefactual.",
   "3": "Cow's milk protein allergy does occur in infants and can cause gastrointestinal bleeding and iron deficiency — but through colitis and blood loss, not autoimmune hemolysis. His indices are those of iron deficiency rather than hemolysis.",
   "4": "Lactose intolerance is uncommon at this age in most populations and causes symptoms without causing iron deficiency anemia. Neither diarrhea nor malabsorption is described here."},
  "Milk crowds out iron-rich food, blocks the absorption of whatever iron he does eat, and can cause slow bleeding from the gut. Fixing the iron without fixing the milk means he will just become deficient again.",
  image="fig_ida_smear",
  imcap="Peripheral blood smear, monolayer.",
  exim="fig_slide_cbc_components",
  excap="The components of the complete blood count, by cell line.")

# ── 14. Transfusing a chronic anemia (§24) ───────────────────────────────────
q("A 3-year-old girl weighing 14 kg is admitted with severe iron deficiency anemia from nutritional deficiency, present for several months. She is alert and playful, with mild tachycardia and a flow murmur, and has no respiratory distress and no bleeding. Her temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 126/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 92/56 mm Hg.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 4.1 g/dL (N=11.5–13.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 12% (N=34%–40%)\nMean corpuscular volume 57 µm3 (N=75–87 µm3)\nSerum ferritin 2 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate transfusion plan?",
  ["A. Transfuse 10 to 15 cc/kg, aiming for a hemoglobin above 12 g/dL",
   "B. Transfuse 10 to 15 cc/kg, aiming for a hemoglobin near 7 g/dL",
   "C. Transfuse 30 cc/kg over 2 hours to normalize the hemoglobin",
   "D. Withhold transfusion and give intravenous iron alone",
   "E. Withhold transfusion and give oral iron alone"],
  1,
  "Two numbers govern this, and they come straight from the case this vignette is modelled on: ROUGHLY 10 TO 15 cc/kg OF RED CELLS RAISES THE HEMOGLOBIN BY ABOUT 2 TO 3 g/dL, and the target is CLOSER TO 7, NOT NORMAL.\n\nThe reason for the modest target is physiologic. In a CHRONIC, SLOWLY DEVELOPING anemia the child has had time to compensate — increased cardiac output, a right-shifted oxygen dissociation curve, expanded plasma volume. She is alert and playful at a hemoglobin of 4.1 g/dL precisely because of that compensation. Rapid correction of a chronically expanded circulation risks volume overload and heart failure, so transfusion is DELIBERATELY PARTIAL AND CAUTIOUS, and THE MARROW DOES THE REST ONCE GIVEN IRON.\n\nThis is the same principle as the restrictive transfusion threshold in adults, applied to a compensated child: transfuse for symptoms and physiologic risk, not to make a number look normal.\n\nThen treat the cause. Oral ferrous sulfate, and — in the toddler with a large milk intake — restriction of cow's milk, which is not an aside but part of the treatment. Expect a reticulocyte response in about a week and roughly 1 g/dL of hemoglobin every 2 to 3 weeks, and continue iron for 3 to 6 months after the hemoglobin normalizes to refill the stores that her ferritin of 2 ng/mL shows are empty.",
  {"0": "This uses the right volume and the wrong target. Driving a chronically anemic, fully compensated child to a normal hemoglobin risks circulatory overload, and it is unnecessary once iron can do the work.",
   "2": "A large, fast volume is what you would give for acute hemorrhage with hypovolemia. She is euvolemic and compensated; this is the transfusion most likely to precipitate heart failure.",
   "3": "Intravenous iron bypasses the gut and would be right for malabsorption or intolerance of oral iron. Neither applies, and at a hemoglobin of 4.1 g/dL with tachycardia she needs red cells now — iron takes weeks to produce them.",
   "4": "Oral iron is the correct definitive treatment and she should receive it. It is not enough on its own at this hemoglobin, since the first measurable response takes about a week."},
  "She has been slowly getting more anemic for months, and her body has adjusted. Filling her back up to normal in one go would flood her circulation. Give just enough blood to be safe, then let iron rebuild the rest.")

# ══════════════════════ §25 · INTERFERENCES AND ARTEFACTS ══════════════════════

# ── 15. Cold agglutinins (§25) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
q("A 79-year-old man is seen in the outpatient clinic in January for follow-up of a recent respiratory illness. He reports no bleeding and no new symptoms, and he looks well. His temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 76/min, and blood pressure is 132/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows no pallor and no jaundice. The sample was drawn in a cold phlebotomy room and transported without warming.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nErythrocyte count 1.9 million/mm3 (N=4.5–5.9 million/mm3)\nHemoglobin 11.8 g/dL (N=13.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 24.3% (N=37%–49%)\nMean corpuscular volume 128 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nMean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration 48.6 g/dL (N=31–37 g/dL)\n\nThe analyzer has flagged the specimen for red cell agglutination. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
  ["A. Redraw the specimen into a citrated tube and repeat",
   "B. Repeat the count using the fluorescent platelet channel",
   "C. Report a macrocytic anemia and measure vitamin B12",
   "D. Transfuse 2 units of packed red blood cells",
   "E. Warm the specimen to 37°C and repeat the analysis"],
  4,
  "The giveaway is the MCHC OF 48.6 g/dL — far above the ceiling of about 37 g/dL, and physically impossible. MCHC is a CONCENTRATION, and a red cell can only hold so much hemoglobin per unit volume, which is what makes it the single best artefact detector on the panel.\n\nThe mechanism runs straight through the calculations. Cold agglutinins clump red cells, so clumped cells cross the aperture TOGETHER AND ARE COUNTED AS ONE VERY LARGE CELL: the red cell count falls and the MCV rises. Because Hct = (RBC × MCV)/10 and the count fell further than the volume rose, the hematocrit is falsely low — and since MCHC = (Hgb/Hct) × 100, the MCHC is driven absurdly high. The HEMOGLOBIN ITSELF IS MEASURED ON LYSED CELLS AND STAYS ACCURATE, which is why it is the one believable number here.\n\nThe fix is to WARM THE SAMPLE TO 37°C AND RE-RUN IT, which disperses the agglutinates. Clinically, follow up with a COLD AGGLUTININ TITRE AND A DIRECT ANTIGLOBULIN TEST — and note the recent respiratory illness, since Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Epstein-Barr virus are classic triggers.\n\nThe habit this section is trying to build: before acting on a startling complete blood count, ask whether the numbers are INTERNALLY CONSISTENT. Does the hematocrit fit the rule of three? Is the MCHC physically possible? Was the tube full, fresh and properly inverted? A great many abnormal counts fail one of those three questions.\n\nThe one genuine exception to the MCHC rule is HEREDITARY SPHEROCYTOSIS, where the cells really are hyperdense — there a high MCHC is a diagnostic clue, not an error.",
  {"0": "A citrated redraw is the fix for EDTA-DEPENDENT PLATELET CLUMPING, where the responsible antibody requires EDTA. Cold agglutinins are temperature-dependent rather than anticoagulant-dependent, so a different tube changes nothing.",
   "2": "This accepts an artefact as a diagnosis. The MCV of 128 µm3 describes clumps counted as single cells, not macrocytes, and the impossible MCHC is the signal that the whole red cell block is unreliable.",
   "1": "The fluorescent channel rescues PLATELET counts that impedance sizing gets wrong — giant platelets missed, microcytes miscounted as platelets. His platelet count is not the problem.",
   "3": "Transfusion would be a response to a hematocrit of 24.3% that is not real. His measured hemoglobin is 11.8 g/dL, he has no symptoms and no pallor, and acting on the calculated value is exactly the harm this section warns about."},
  "The cold made his red cells stick together in clumps, and the machine counted each clump as one huge cell. That threw off the arithmetic and produced a hemoglobin concentration that is physically impossible. Warm the sample and run it again.",
  exim="fig_agglutination",
  excap="Red cell agglutination — clumps that the analyzer counts as single large cells.")

# ── 16. Pseudothrombocytopenia (§25) ─────────────────────────────────────────
q("A 44-year-old woman has a routine complete blood count before an elective hernia repair. She feels well, takes no medications, and has had no bruising, no petechiae, no gum bleeding, and no heavy menstrual periods. She has had dental extractions and a prior cesarean delivery without bleeding problems. Her temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 72/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Physical examination shows no petechiae, no purpura, and no splenomegaly.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 13.4 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 40% (N=36%–46%)\nLeukocyte count 7,100/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 22,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\n\nReview of the peripheral smear shows large aggregates of platelets, with adequate numbers of platelets within the aggregates. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
  ["A. Obtain a bone marrow aspiration and biopsy",
   "B. Redraw the sample in a citrated tube and repeat",
   "C. Transfuse one apheresis unit of platelets before surgery",
   "D. Treat with intravenous immunoglobulin and a corticosteroid",
   "E. Warm the specimen to 37°C and repeat the count"],
  1,
  "This is PSEUDOTHROMBOCYTOPENIA — EDTA-dependent platelet clumping — and THE CLUE IS THE ABSENCE OF BLEEDING. A platelet count of 22,000/mm3 in an entirely asymptomatic outpatient should not be believed before it is checked. Genuine severe thrombocytopenia usually shows petechiae or mucosal bleeding; a very low count with NO bleeding at all is the signature of clumping.\n\nThe mechanism is the impedance size window again: clumps are TOO LARGE to be counted as platelets and are missed, or are misread as white cells — which is why clumping can falsely RAISE the white count as well. The analyzer usually flags it as 'Platelet clumps?'\n\nThe smear confirms it directly, as it has here. The fix is to REDRAW IN CITRATE, the blue-top tube, because the responsible antibody is EDTA-DEPENDENT. A fluorescent platelet count also helps, since it identifies platelets by staining rather than by size.\n\nWhy this matters enough to be its own topic: ACTING ON THE FALSE NUMBER IS THE REAL HARM. It triggers a hematology referral, a marrow biopsy, or a platelet transfusion the patient never needed — and in a preoperative patient, it can cancel or delay an operation for no reason.\n\nThe tube logic underneath: EDTA binds calcium IRREVERSIBLY, which is fine for counting intact cells. Citrate binds calcium REVERSIBLY, which is why coagulation testing uses it — the laboratory adds calcium back to restart clotting on demand. Same principle, opposite requirement, different tube.",
  {"0": "A marrow examination is exactly the unnecessary invasive workup this artefact generates. There is no cytopenia in the other lines, no bleeding, and a smear that already explains the number.",
   "2": "Transfusion for a count of 22,000/mm3 before surgery would be defensible if the count were real. It exposes her to reactions, alloimmunization and transfusion-transmitted infection for a laboratory artefact.",
   "3": "Immunoglobulin and steroids treat immune thrombocytopenia, which is the diagnosis this count would suggest if taken at face value. The smear shows aggregates rather than a genuinely low platelet mass.",
   "4": "Warming to 37°C disperses COLD AGGLUTININS, which clump RED cells and produce a low red cell count with an impossibly high MCHC. Her red cell indices are normal; the antibody here is EDTA-dependent, not cold-dependent."},
  "Her platelets clumped together inside the collection tube because of the chemical in it. The machine cannot count a clump, so the number came back dangerously low — but she has no bruising at all, which is the giveaway. Redraw into a different tube.",
  exim="fig_slide_methods",
  excap="The counting technologies side by side. Impedance assigns identity by size, which is why anything the wrong size is counted as the wrong thing.")

# ── 17. Microcytes counted as platelets (§25) ────────────────────────────────
q("A 33-year-old woman with severe iron deficiency anemia from menorrhagia has a complete blood count before starting therapy. She reports fatigue but no bruising and no thrombotic symptoms. Her temperature is 36.9°C (98.4°F), pulse is 94/min, and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 7.2 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 22% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 61 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 19.8% (N=11.4%–14.5%)\nSerum ferritin 3 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\nPlatelet count 610,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\n\nReview of the peripheral smear shows marked microcytosis with only moderate numbers of platelets, far fewer than the reported count suggests. Which of the following best explains the discrepancy?",
  ["A. Giant platelets falling outside the impedance size window",
   "B. Microcytic red cells falling within the platelet size window",
   "C. Nucleated red cells counted within the platelet channel",
   "D. Platelet clumping from an EDTA-dependent antibody",
   "E. Reactive thrombocytosis driven by the iron deficiency itself"],
  1,
  "Impedance counting assigns identity BY SIZE, and there is a separate aperture for each population — a small pore for platelets, a mid-size one for red cells, a larger one for white cells. The consequence is the single idea that generates most of this section: ANYTHING THAT IS THE WRONG SIZE FOR ITS IDENTITY IS COUNTED AS THE WRONG THING.\n\n• TOO SMALL → counted as a platelet. Schistocytes, MICROCYTES and white cell fragments falsely RAISE the platelet count.\n• TOO BIG TO BE A PLATELET → missed. Platelet clumps and giant platelets falsely LOWER it.\n\nIn severe iron deficiency the red cells become small enough to fall into the platelet size window, which is what has happened here at an MCV of 61 µm3. The smear is the check that catches it — the reported count and the visible platelets do not match.\n\nResolution is to count by a method that does NOT depend on size: the FLUORESCENT PLATELET CHANNEL, which stains platelet rough endoplasmic reticulum and mitochondria with oxazine and identifies platelets by their CONTENTS. It correlates well with CD41/CD61 flow cytometry and is specifically useful at the extremes of the count.\n\nThe clinical stake is real in both directions: a falsely high platelet count in an iron-deficient patient can prompt an unnecessary workup for thrombocytosis, while a falsely low one can trigger an unnecessary transfusion.",
  {"0": "Giant platelets are too LARGE for the platelet window, so they are missed and the count reads falsely LOW — the opposite error. That pattern should also raise the question of Bernard-Soulier syndrome.",
   "2": "Nucleated red cells have nuclei, so they land in the LEUKOCYTE gate and falsely raise the WHITE cell count. Modern analyzers count and subtract them automatically.",
   "3": "EDTA-dependent clumping makes platelets too large to be sized as platelets, so they are missed or counted as leukocytes — again a falsely LOW platelet count, and the smear would show aggregates rather than a sparse field.",
   "4": "Iron deficiency genuinely does cause reactive thrombocytosis, which makes this the strongest distractor and a real competing explanation. What excludes it is the smear: a true thrombocytosis shows abundant platelets on the slide, and hers are only moderate in number."},
  "The machine decides what a cell is mostly by size. Her red cells have shrunk so much that the counter mistakes them for platelets, so it reports far more platelets than she actually has.",
  exim="fig_slide_coulter",
  excap="The Coulter principle — pulse height is size, and size is how the analyzer decides what a cell is.")

# ── 18. Nucleated red cells in the white count (§25) ─────────────────────────
q("A 68-year-old man with primary myelofibrosis is seen for routine follow-up. He reports early satiety and fatigue but no fever, no cough, and no dysuria. His temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 86/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a spleen palpable 8 cm below the left costal margin.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.2 g/dL (N=13.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 28% (N=37%–49%)\nLeukocyte count 22,400/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 118,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\n\nReview of the peripheral smear shows tear-drop cells and 46 nucleated erythrocytes per 100 leukocytes. The differential shows no increase in immature granulocytes, and the patient has no clinical evidence of infection. Which of the following best explains the leukocyte count?",
  ["A. Lipemia adding absorbance at the reading wavelength",
   "B. Nucleated erythrocytes counted within the leukocyte gate",
   "C. Platelet clumps large enough to be counted as leukocytes",
   "D. Red cell agglutination reducing the erythrocyte count",
   "E. White cell fragments counted within the platelet channel"],
  1,
  "The analyzer COUNTS NUCLEATED RED CELLS AS WHITE CELLS unless it corrects for them, because IT IS LOOKING FOR NUCLEI. With 46 nucleated erythrocytes per 100 leukocytes, a substantial share of his reported 22,400/mm3 is not leukocytes at all. Modern analyzers count and subtract them automatically; otherwise the white count is corrected manually.\n\nThe underlying point is worth carrying: A NUCLEATED RED CELL IN THE BLOOD IS A CELL RELEASED BEFORE IT WAS FINISHED. Either the marrow is under stress and rushing output, or something is TAKING OVER THE MARROW AND FORCING CELLS OUT — fibrosis, metastasis. Either way it needs explaining. Here it is explained: primary myelofibrosis, with the TEAR-DROP CELLS that go with it — cells squeezed as they exit a scarred marrow — and massive splenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis.\n\nWhy red cells have nuclei in the first place closes the loop. A red cell is born with one because it needs it to MANUFACTURE ITS HEMOGLOBIN; once fully loaded, the nucleus has no further use and gets in the way, so the cell ejects it. A reticulocyte is the stage just after that — no nucleus, but leftover RNA. A mature red cell has neither.\n\nThe same fact explains something from an earlier section: G6PD deficiency behaves as it does because a red cell with no nucleus CANNOT MAKE MORE ENZYME, so the G6PD it carries at birth is all it will ever have.",
  {"0": "Lipemia adds turbidity and absorbance at 555 nm, so it falsely raises the measured HEMOGLOBIN and therefore the MCH and MCHC. It does not touch the leukocyte count.",
   "2": "Platelet clumps can be misread as leukocytes and are a genuine cause of a falsely raised white count, so this is a real mechanism. The smear here shows nucleated red cells rather than aggregates, and clumping would also drop the platelet count further than seen.",
   "3": "Red cell agglutination lowers the erythrocyte count and inflates the MCV, producing an impossibly high MCHC. It is a red cell artefact and does not raise the white count.",
   "4": "White cell fragments are small enough to fall into the PLATELET window, so they falsely raise the platelet count. That is the mirror-image error and affects a different line."},
  "His marrow is scarred, so it pushes out red cells before they have thrown away their nucleus. The counter identifies white cells by looking for nuclei, so it counts those unfinished red cells as white cells.",
  exim="fig_nrbc",
  excap="A nucleated red blood cell on the smear — a cell released before it finished, and one the analyzer will count as a leukocyte.")

# ── 19. Lipemia and the hemoglobin reading (§25) ─────────────────────────────
q("A 58-year-old man in the intensive care unit is receiving parenteral nutrition with a lipid emulsion after a small bowel resection. He is hemodynamically stable and has had no bleeding. His temperature is 37.1°C (98.8°F), pulse is 88/min, and blood pressure is 122/74 mm Hg. The laboratory notes that the plasma in the separated specimen is grossly turbid and milky.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nErythrocyte count 3.8 million/mm3 (N=4.5–5.9 million/mm3)\nHemoglobin 16.8 g/dL (N=13.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 34% (N=37%–49%)\nMean corpuscular volume 89 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nMean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration 49.4 g/dL (N=31–37 g/dL)\nSerum triglycerides 1,840 mg/dL (N=<150 mg/dL)\n\nWhich of the following best explains these findings?",
  ["A. Cold agglutinins clumping red cells before analysis",
   "B. Hereditary spherocytosis producing hyperdense erythrocytes",
   "C. Lipemia falsely raising the measured hemoglobin",
   "D. Polycythemia from hemoconcentration and volume depletion",
   "E. Specimen clotting before the sample reached the analyzer"],
  2,
  "Hemoglobin is measured PHOTOMETRICALLY: sodium lauryl sulfate lyses the cells, the heme is oxidised, SLS binds it into a stable coloured complex, and ABSORBANCE IS READ AT 555 nm — a wavelength chosen specifically to minimise interference from turbidity. Minimise, not eliminate.\n\nAnything that ADDS ABSORBANCE AT THAT WAVELENGTH inflates the measured hemoglobin: LIPEMIA, marked leukocytosis, and HYPERBILIRUBINEMIA. And because MCH and MCHC are calculated FROM the hemoglobin, both go up with it. His triglycerides of 1,840 mg/dL and grossly milky plasma are the cause, and his MCHC of 49.4 g/dL is the alarm bell.\n\nThat is the second of the two routes to an impossible MCHC, and the distinction between them is exactly what this question tests:\n• THE HEMOGLOBIN WAS READ TOO HIGH — lipemia, very high white count, hyperbilirubinemia. The red cell count and MCV are unaffected.\n• THE HEMATOCRIT WAS CALCULATED TOO LOW — cold agglutinins clumping red cells, which drops the count and raises the MCV.\n\nHis MCV is normal at 89 µm3 and there is no agglutination flag, which points at the numerator rather than the denominator. Note too that his hemoglobin of 16.8 g/dL against a hematocrit of 34% badly breaks the rule of three in the upward direction — another internal-consistency check failing.\n\nThe fix is PLASMA REPLACEMENT OR BLANK CORRECTION, in which the specimen is centrifuged, the turbid plasma removed and replaced with saline, and the hemoglobin re-measured.",
  {"0": "Cold agglutinins also produce an impossible MCHC, which makes this the main alternative to exclude. They do it by clumping cells, so the red cell count falls and the MCV RISES — his MCV is normal and the analyzer has raised no agglutination flag.",
   "1": "Hereditary spherocytosis is the one genuine exception to the MCHC rule, because the cells really are hyperdense — a high MCHC there is a clue rather than an error. It runs closer to 37 to 38 g/dL, not 49, and comes with spherocytes, reticulocytosis and hemolysis.",
   "3": "Hemoconcentration raises the hemoglobin AND the hematocrit together, since both reflect the same red cells in less plasma. His hematocrit is low at 34%, so the two are moving in opposite directions.",
   "4": "A clotted specimen traps cells so that they never reach the aperture, and every count comes back falsely LOW with no flag to explain it. That would not inflate the hemoglobin or the MCHC."},
  "Hemoglobin is measured by shining light through the sample. His blood is cloudy with fat from the feeding solution, and the cloudiness blocks light just like hemoglobin does — so the machine reports more hemoglobin than is really there.",
  exim="fig_slide_sls",
  excap="Hemoglobin by sodium lauryl sulfate: lyse, oxidise, complex, then read absorbance at 555 nm.")

# ── 20. The uninverted tube (§25) ────────────────────────────────────────────
q("A 52-year-old woman has a complete blood count drawn during a routine visit. She feels well, takes no medications, and has no bruising, no infections, and no fatigue. Her temperature is 36.9°C (98.4°F), pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 120/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows no pallor, no petechiae, and no lymphadenopathy. The phlebotomist recalls that the lavender-top tube was filled, set upright in the rack without mixing, and sent to the laboratory an hour later.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 29% (N=36%–46%)\nLeukocyte count 2,100/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 46,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\n\nA complete blood count 4 months ago was entirely normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
  ["A. Accept the result and arrange a bone marrow biopsy",
   "B. Perform a fluorescent platelet count on the same specimen",
   "C. Reject the specimen and redraw",
   "D. Report the counts with a comment about possible clotting",
   "E. Warm the specimen to 37°C and repeat the analysis"],
  2,
  "This is PARTIAL CLOTTING FROM INADEQUATE MIXING, and the correct response is to REJECT AND REDRAW. 'No clots and no clumps' is an EXPLICIT SPECIMEN ACCEPTANCE CRITERION, not a preference.\n\nThe mechanism is in how the tube is made. EDTA is SPRAYED ON THE INSIDE SURFACES of the tube, including near the top. Blood that simply runs to the bottom NEVER MEETS THE FULL DOSE of anticoagulant, and partial clotting follows. The phlebotomist's several gentle inversions are what prevent this.\n\nThe clot then SEQUESTERS PLATELETS, AND SOME WHITE AND RED CELLS WITH THEM. Those cells are no longer in the liquid being analysed, so EVERY COUNT COMES BACK FALSELY LOW — which is precisely the pancytopenia reported here in a well woman whose count was normal 4 months ago.\n\nThe danger is that the result is PLAUSIBLE. A pancytopenia in a real patient invites a marrow biopsy, a hematology referral, or a transfusion — for an artefact. An artefact that reads as a disease is more dangerous than one that reads as nonsense, because nothing about the number itself announces the problem.\n\nThe rest of the specimen requirements, for completeness: fresh whole blood in EDTA, ideally within 3 HOURS of collection and acceptable within 8 HOURS, since cells swell over time and the MCV becomes unreliable. Refrigerated samples beyond that may degrade.",
  {"0": "Acting on the number is the specific harm this section warns about. A marrow biopsy for an artefactual pancytopenia is invasive, expensive and frightening, and the history of an unmixed tube explains the whole panel.",
   "1": "The fluorescent channel solves SIZE-based platelet errors — clumps, giant platelets, microcytes counted as platelets. It cannot recover cells that are physically trapped in a clot and never reached the analyzer.",
   "3": "Releasing the result with a caveat leaves a falsely alarming set of numbers in the record, where they may be acted on by someone who does not read the comment. The specimen fails an acceptance criterion outright.",
   "4": "Warming disperses COLD AGGLUTININS, which affect red cells and produce a high MCHC. The problem here is mechanical — cells trapped in fibrin — and no amount of warming returns them to the suspension."},
  "The tube has anticoagulant coating its inside walls, and it only works if you gently turn the tube over a few times. This one was not mixed, so the blood partly clotted and the clot swallowed up cells — making every count look low in a perfectly healthy person.")
